//! Thailand's national highways (Department of Highways, AADT 2021 table: vehicle-km a year of
//! 2,695 control sections in ten classes and motorcycles, each section with its province) as daily
//! traffic on their OSM rows. Generated by `th-doh-table.py` and `gen-th-highways.py` (private
//! evidence 2026-10-03, thai-highways). A highway's vehicle-km in a province over the length of its
//! OSM rows there (motorway to tertiary and unclassified rows whose first numeric ref is the
//! highway; a one-way carriageway half) is the mean traffic across both directions: highway 1
//! carries 124,800 vehicles a day in Pathum Thani and 11,900 in Chai Nat, where one mean put 26,500
//! on both. Against the department's 2025 counts at 61 stations of 34 highways in Nakhon Sawan (a
//! published extract of its station table, which is not reachable from abroad) the province's means
//! miss by 1.3 dB on average (highway 1 there 21,500 counted, 20,300 the province's mean, 26,500
//! the highway's; 117 24,000, 26,000 and 10,800), the highway's means by 1.5 dB; 2021's vehicle-km
//! read them 0.2 dB low, 2019's 0.5 dB high. Rows the sections do not cover keep their estimates,
//! each check a 3-MAD outlier rule over the country: a highway whose vehicle-km over the world
//! major-road model's expectation for its rows lies outside 0.23-26.9, or whose OSM km per control
//! section lies outside 0.82-240 (an OSM ref on a fragment, or on a road the department's section
//! is not: Bangkok's Lat Phrao road carries "336", the department's 336 is its interchange); a
//! province whose km per section or whose vehicle-km over the expectation departs from its
//! highway's by more than 3.2 or 2.8 times (a section listed under the neighbouring province:
//! highway 333 has 4.5 km in Nakhon Sawan for two sections of 28 km); a province where the
//! department lists no section of the highway. 1,675 highway-provinces cover 50,145 of the 53,510
//! km of rows carrying a department number. Category shares: light (cars, vans, small buses, small
//! trucks), medium (medium buses and trucks), heavy (large buses and trucks, trailers,
//! semi-trailers), motorcycles.

